you have other processes which  contribute to this final state  namely the one where you have the four  leptons coming  from the zebra then itself and or  um from a pair of the bosons is shown  here  there's also processes which mimic the  leptons  in the detector those have to be  evaluated as well and they're typically  shown here in this plot in green  okay so as you can imagine we can  measure the cross-section very precisely  because we can identify those particles  well but we can also measure the mass  and this is shown here  the summary of measurements from atlas  and cms using  those two final states the one with two  photons or the one with four leptons  and you see that measurements are  generally in agreement  and they can be combined to this  measurement of 125 gv  and the best the combined value is shown  here  so this is a precision measurement and  since the exposure mass now is the only  unknown  parameter of the higgs in the standard  model we we know  and can check all other properties  and one is you know the coupling  strengths to the both on which we just  looked at already  or the coupling strength to the fermions  so this is two examples one  it's showing hicks to taut events  top last minus and you see again there's  a lot of  background processes which mimic have  the same signature the signal  but we can find when we subtract the  background from the data  the nexus of event again at 125 gb  so towers themselves decay and the decay  into neutrinos which cannot be measured  at this detectors  are therefore the mass reconstruction is  much harder than in the final stage we  discussed before  that is easier in this final stage here  where we have a higgs into two moons  in the plus c minus the issue here is  that the rate  is very small the number of fixed bosons  decaying to be  very small but there's also a large  amount of background so again very  similar to the hicks to gamma gamma  finance that we looked before we have a  huge amount of background  and here by eyes you don't even see the  bump you see the bump a little bit  uh when you do data minus background  subtraction  so you see this small axis here which is  consistent with redundant standard  deviation so  the likelihood of the background to  fluctuate without the presence of the  hex signal  is about three standard deviation all  right this information then can be used  to interact information about the  couplings itself and this is a beautiful  plot here  which shows the x coupling on one axis  either to the fermions or the bodon  versus the mass of the particle  and so you find your favorite particles  the top the w  z the bottom the tower and the muon  those are the ones where we can actually  measure the coupling um and you see  whether or not this is an agreement with  a standard model which is this  blue dotted line here and you see it is  and that plot here tells me that the  higgs mechanism  is responsible responsible for the mass  generation of this particle  so it might be that there is a mechanism  which is very similar to the higgs boson  which results in the very same  observations in nature  um but it's not quite what we have in  the standard model in order to make  those statements  one has to improve the size  of the error bars here or the  statistical significance or the  significance of the measurements  that is part of our program that's what  we're trying to do  all right in summary again this is a  very high level summary so we measured  the mass  we measured the spin and cp of exposure  we measured the coupling to the z the w  the top the bottom the tau and the muon  we have not been able to measure the  coupling to lighter quark masses  strange charm up and down  we have not been able to measure the  coupling to the lx one  that would be spectacular and we have  not measured the coupling to itself so  in the standard model the higgs boson  can couple to itself  so we have diagrams which look like this  x x x and we have not been able to  measure this  this is not free in the standard model  and this would be you know  closing the argument that the higgs  boson requires mass  or the higgs boson is the particle  predicted in the standard model  giving mass to w and z goes on  if you want you can the strength of the  coupling here is this lambda  this is the lambda term in our potential  so that's what we are trying to measure  in the future  but there's more open questions um maybe  there's more than  one exposure on the higgs adding one  duplet to the standard model with this  potential  is one possible solution to the problem  of generating mars  but you could have very well added two  or n  or triplets or more complicated things  and so the question is  are those maybe realized in nature or  not  um  will more precision tell us something  about the higgs boson  are there the case of the higgs bosons  of non-standard model particles for  example the higgs boson decaying into  those guys here which could be evidence  for dark matter  we have looks we have not seen this but  more precision might give us a different  answer  so that's basically the status or the  summary of where we are with the higgs  boson again much more can be said about  that  and you will see this every now and then  in seminars at mit  or other places  you

